  <p>
    Reading aloud is where the thinking habit starts, because it is the one place
    a child gets to interrupt. A child who sits and listens is learning to
    receive. A child who asks a question mid-story is learning to push back, and
    that is the whole skill. The books I write are built around a boy who asks
    what is underneath things. This guide is how you read them, or any book, so
    your kid starts asking too.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the question is worth more than the answer</h2>
  <p>
    Most parents do the same thing at the end of a book. They close the cover and
    ask what happened, what did we learn. That is a quiz. It trains a child to
    remember, which is fine, but remembering is not thinking. A question that
    comes from the child, out of nowhere, in the middle of the story, is worth
    more than ten quiz answers, because the child made it themselves. That is the
    habit that survives.
  </p>

  <h2>The one mistake that kills questions</h2>
  <p>
    The mistake is answering too fast and asking too much. When a child asks why
    the bear is sad, the adult impulse is to explain the whole thing. Stop. You
    are about to do the thinking for them. And when the book ends, resist the
    quiz. A child who expects to be tested at the end of every story learns to
    read with one eye on the test. That is not reading. That is compliance.
  </p>

  <h2>Three moves that make a read-aloud produce questions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pause before the answer.</strong> Read a page, then close your mouth
    and wait. Give the child time to notice what you noticed. Silence is where
    the question forms. Count to five before you turn the page.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ask the question you actually wonder about.</strong> Not the test
    question. The genuine one. I do not know why the dark is scary, and I have
    told my son so. When you ask a question you do not know the answer to, you
    are modeling what a question is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Leave one question open.</strong> Do not wrap everything up. The best
    read-alouds end with a question nobody answers, and the child carries it to
    bed. That is the point of our whole series. The questions matter whether the
    answers ever come.
  </p>

  <h2>Three reading styles, and what each one trains</h2>
  <p>
    You are always using one of these. It is worth knowing which one, because
    they train different children.
  </p>
