  <p>
    Performance is not wrong. Every kid deserves a good read-aloud performance.
    The mistake is making it the only style, because then the child never learns
    to do anything but sit. Add the question style and the same books do double
    duty.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with a question you cannot answer</h2>
  <p>
    A child will ask things you do not know, and things nobody knows. The worst
    thing you can do is fake an answer, because the child can feel it, and it
    teaches them that a question is only worth asking if you already know the
    answer. The honest move is the best one. Say, I do not know. What do you
    think? You are giving the question back to them, which is exactly where it
    belongs. That is the Socratic move, and it is the engine of the Curious Kid
    books.
  </p>

  <h2>It gets easier the more you do it</h2>
  <p>
    At first, pausing feels strange and the silence is awkward. A child used to
    being performed at will sit and wait for you to start again. Keep waiting.
    The first time a real question comes out of them, unprompted, in the middle
    of the story, you will know why the silence was worth it. The habit builds
    fast, because a question of their own is more interesting than being read to.
  </p>

